            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Define “Scalable” and “Convertible” First</h2>
<p>A creative is <strong>scalable</strong> when it passes review and maintains stable delivery, impressions, clicks, or add-to-cart actions. It is <strong>convertible</strong> when it generates qualified conversions and supports the required profit margin.</p>
<p>Do not treat ROAS as the only verdict. High-ticket products, subscription businesses, and new-customer campaigns should be reviewed against their own conversion windows and economics. Classify creatives into three groups:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Delivery without conversion</strong>: Click-through performance is acceptable, but conversion is weak. Scaling may simply increase losses.</li>
<li><strong>Conversion without scale</strong>: The creative converts well but cannot maintain delivery because of narrow audiences, policy restrictions, or fatigue.</li>
<li><strong>Scalable and convertible</strong>: Delivery, conversion quality, and profitability meet the campaign standard.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>Treat traffic acquisition as a process metric and profitable conversion as the outcome metric.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Creative Review Checklist: Diagnose the Funnel</h2>
<h3>1. Check Account and Tracking Health</h3>
<ul>
<li>Verify the ad account, Pixel, Conversions API, and event mapping. Rule out duplicated or missing conversions.</li>
<li>Check for narrow audiences, repeated learning resets, abrupt budget changes, and incompatible bidding strategies.</li>
<li>Review placement coverage, mobile rendering, and policy restrictions.</li>
<li>Confirm that attribution windows are complete and organic conversions are not mixed into paid results.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Check Whether the Creative Attracts the Right Users</h3>
<p>Focus on the first seconds of the video, thumbnail, opening frame, headline, and CTA. Weak CTR often indicates a mismatch between the offer, visual message, and audience. Changing the audience should not be the only solution.</p>
<p>For example, a UGC video for a utility app may generate strong clicks but weak sign-ups because the landing page does not match the ad promise. The team should improve message continuity and the registration flow before increasing spend.</p>
<h3>3. Check Conversion Quality</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confirm that conversion events represent real business goals, such as completed purchases, qualified trials, or valid leads.</li>
<li>Exclude duplicate submissions, test orders, abnormal regions, low-quality forms, and invalid clicks.</li>
<li>Review refunds, cancellations, received revenue, and gross margin.</li>
<li>Allow sufficient observation time for longer conversion cycles before declaring a creative ineffective.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Check Spend Against the Profit Model</h3>
<p>Calculate cost per qualified conversion, not just cost per purchase. A cross-border e-commerce campaign may appear profitable on ROAS while remaining negative after refunds, discounts, fulfillment, and creative production costs.</p>
<p>Use a simple grading system:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Grade A</strong>: Stable delivery, acceptable qualified conversion cost, and room to scale.</li>
<li><strong>Grade B</strong>: Strong results in selected areas, but sample size, continuity, or profitability still requires validation.</li>
<li><strong>Grade C</strong>: Normal delivery but weak conversion. Keep on a limited test budget.</li>
<li><strong>Grade D</strong>: Clearly inefficient or unable to maintain delivery. Move it to the retirement pool.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Weekly Creative Review SOP</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Standardize the criteria</strong>: Align business goals, attribution windows, budget ranges, and creative labels across teams.</li>
<li><strong>Export the data</strong>: Aggregate spend, impressions, clicks, conversions, qualified conversions, revenue, refunds, and actual costs by creative.</li>
<li><strong>Grade in layers</strong>: Assess delivery stability first, conversion quality second, and profit potential last.</li>
<li><strong>Isolate variables</strong>: Test one key element at a time, such as the opening, claim, proof point, use case, or CTA.</li>
<li><strong>Take action</strong>: Scale Grade A creatives and create variations; validate Grade B; reduce spend on Grade C; stop Grade D.</li>
<li><strong>Document decisions</strong>: Record why each creative was retained, revised, or retired in the naming system and review sheet.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Three Common Mistakes</h3>
<p><strong>Does a high ROAS always justify scaling?</strong> No. Refund rates, gross margin, and attribution integrity must also be checked.</p>
<p><strong>Should a creative with low CTR but high CVR continue running?</strong> It can be tested, but the team must confirm an acceptable acquisition cost and whether it can scale.</p>
<p><strong>Does falling delivery always mean creative fatigue?</strong> Not necessarily. Check audience saturation, auction changes, tracking issues, seasonality, and audience overlap before replacing the asset.</p>
<h2>Final Promotion Checklist</h2>
<p>Before moving a creative into the scaling pool, confirm that events are healthy, delivery is stable, traffic quality is acceptable, qualified conversions meet the target, profit is viable, the landing journey matches the ad, and the next test variable is clearly defined. Scalability determines whether a creative has a chance to be seen. Conversion and profitability determine whether sustained investment makes sense.</p>
